### card homework-3 "Problem Set 3"
	
	*Due Thursday, September 24th at 11:59 PM*
	
	Complete the following problems and submit them as a PDF to Gradescope. You should show enough work that there is no question about the mathematical process used to obtain your answers, and so that your peers in the class could easily follow along. I encourage you to collaborate with your classmates, so long as you write up your solutions independently. Using AI tools to assist with the homework is not permitted.

	- - -

	#. Find an equation of a line passing through the points $(5, 1, 2)$ and $(3, 4, 1)$.

	**Solution:** We subtract one point from the other to find the direction vector:

	$$
		\vec{v} = \left< 3 - 5,\ 4 - 1,\ 1 - 2 \right> = \left< -2, 3, -1 \right>.
	$$

	Taking $(5, 1, 2)$ as the point (either one works!), the line is

	$$
		\vec{r}(t) = \left< 5, 1, 2 \right> + t\left< -2, 3, -1 \right> = \left< 5 - 2t,\ 1 + 3t,\ 2 - t \right>.
	$$

	We can check that $\vec{r}(0) = \left< 5, 1, 2 \right>$ and $\vec{r}(1) = \left< 3, 4, 1 \right>$, so both points really are on the line.

	#. Find an equation of the plane containing the points $(1, 7, 0)$, $(-3, 1, 1)$, and $(2, 4, 0)$.

	**Solution:** The vectors from $(1, 7, 0)$ to the other two points are

	$$
		\vec{v} &= \left< -3 - 1,\ 1 - 7,\ 1 - 0 \right> = \left< -4, -6, 1 \right>

		\vec{w} &= \left< 2 - 1,\ 4 - 7,\ 0 - 0 \right> = \left< 1, -3, 0 \right>,
	$$

	both of which lie in the plane. Their cross product is a normal vector:

	$$
		\vec{n} = \vec{v} \times \vec{w} &= \left< 3, 1, 18 \right>
	$$

	Then the plane equation is

	$$
		3(x - 1) - (y - 7) + 18z = 0,
	$$

	or equivalently $3x - y + 18z = -4$.

	#. Find the intersection of the line from question #-2 and the plane from question #-1 if it exists, or show they don't intersect.

	**Solution:** To attempt to find the point, we plug the components of the line into the equation of the plane and solve for the $t$ that lands us there:

	$$
		3(5 - 2t) - (1 + 3t) + 18(2 - t) &= -4

		15 - 6t - 1 - 3t + 36 - 18t &= -4

		-27t &= -54

		t &= 2.
	$$

	Plugging that back into $\vec{r}(t)$ gives the point of intersection:

	$$
		\vec{r}\left( 2 \right) = \left< 1, 7, 0 \right>,
	$$

	which we already know is on the plane!

